WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.030
Before you write any code, draw the map.

2
00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.617
Supplier files arrive by SFTP.

3
00:00:05.637 --> 00:00:07.030
Clicks pour out of the app.

4
00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.240
And orders live in MySQL.

5
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.790
Three ideas: the shape of the pipeline, the network, and the keys.

6
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.475
First, two words: batch and streaming.

7
00:00:16.495 --> 00:00:18.100
Batch is like the post.

8
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.910
Letters are collected, then delivered together, on a schedule.

9
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.760
Streaming is like a phone call.

10
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.295
Each event is handled as it happens.

11
00:00:26.315 --> 00:00:28.470
Next: ETL or ELT?

12
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.180
It is only a question of when you transform.

13
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.955
ELT loads the raw data into BigQuery first, then transforms it with SQL.

14
00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:37.640
It is the default.

15
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:37.990
Why?

16
00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.427
The raw copy stays.

17
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:42.075
So you can fix a bug, and run it all again.

18
00:00:42.095 --> 00:00:46.560
The classic streaming path: Pub/Sub, then Dataflow, then BigQuery.

19
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.795
And for database changes: Datastream, straight into BigQuery.

20
00:00:50.815 --> 00:00:53.858
The trap: devices writing straight into the database.

21
00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:56.595
Always buffer with Pub/Sub.

22
00:00:56.615 --> 00:00:57.882
Second: the network.

23
00:00:57.902 --> 00:00:59.880
Workers need a way to talk.

24
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.643
Secure workers have no public IP address.

25
00:01:02.663 --> 00:01:05.315
So how do they reach Google's services?

26
00:01:05.335 --> 00:01:08.152
Turn on Private Google Access on the subnet.

27
00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:11.200
That is the private door to Google APIs.

28
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.710
And if they need the wider internet, add Cloud NAT.

29
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:18.735
To reach Cloud SQL, use its private IP, or the Auth Proxy.

30
00:01:18.755 --> 00:01:21.145
Never open it to the whole internet.

31
00:01:21.165 --> 00:01:22.033
One trap.

32
00:01:22.053 --> 00:01:25.655
Dataflow workers are stuck, and cannot talk to each other.

33
00:01:25.675 --> 00:01:32.005
A firewall rule is missing, for ports 12345 and 12346.

34
00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:33.815
Third: the keys.

35
00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:36.652
Data moving between services is encrypted for you.

36
00:01:36.672 --> 00:01:38.200
Nothing to do.

37
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.510
For data at rest, put your own key, your CMEK, on every hop.

38
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:47.835
That means the Pub/Sub topic, the Dataflow job, the bucket, and the table.

39
00:01:47.855 --> 00:01:49.095
Recap.

40
00:01:49.115 --> 00:01:51.757
Land raw data first, and transform later.

41
00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:53.955
Buffer streams with Pub/Sub.

42
00:01:53.975 --> 00:01:56.605
Private workers need Private Google Access.

43
00:01:56.625 --> 00:01:58.915
And your key goes on every hop.

44
00:01:58.935 --> 00:02:01.935
Now try the checkpoint below.
